; pi4CoreWitness.pi4 - the bounded secondary-core witness.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. IT DOES NOT SHIP. It is an instrument, not a how-to.
;
;   pmfc RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4CoreWitness.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x500000 --stack-addr 0x4F00000 --entry-returns \
;        -o corewitness.img
;
; ======================================================================
;  IT RELEASES NO CORE. THERE IS NO PATH IN THIS FILE THAT DOES.
; ======================================================================
;  Nothing here writes a firmware spin slot, sends an event, or branches
;  a secondary anywhere. The four slots at $D8/$E0/$E8/$F0 are READ and
;  reported and never written; grep this file for `PokeI` and every hit
;  is inside this program's own BSS.
;
;  That is not caution for its own sake. Three separate facts each make a
;  release from a PAYLOAD wrong, and any one of them alone settles it:
;
;  1. A RELEASED CORE PARKS IN THE CODE THAT RELEASED IT. The park loop
;     is two instructions inside the released routine, and a payload's
;     image is memory the monitor stages the NEXT payload into. The core
;     cannot be recalled, so the day something else is loaded at this
;     address, a parked core is sitting in a `wfe` loop that has become
;     somebody else's data - and one stray SEV executes it. The resident
;     monitor is the only correct home for a permanent worker, which is
;     what RaspberryPi4/Lib/core_worker.pi4's own header says.
;
;  2. `CoreRawPrepare()`'s running-image overlap guard asks
;     `HwMonLo()/HwMonHi()`, and those are derived from
;     `__image_start__`/`__image_end__` OF THE IMAGE THAT CONTAINS THEM.
;     Called from a payload it measures the PAYLOAD and reports that a
;     secondary stack clear of the payload is clear of the monitor too.
;     That is a confident wrong answer with no symptom, so this file does
;     not call it at all.
;
;  3. ON THE BOARD AS IT BOOTS TODAY IT WOULD BE REFUSED ANYWAY. The
;     stick boots KERNEL8.IMG under the FIRMWARE'S OWN stub, which erets
;     to EL2. Every secondary parked by that stub is parked at EL2, and
;     both halves of the saved bootstrap accept only the pinned EL3
;     contract. The expected result of running this witness today is
;     status 1 - "not at EL3" - and that is a measurement, not a failure.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IT DOES PROVE, ON THE SILICON
; ======================================================================
;  * The exception level the monitor is actually running at, and the
;    SCTLR of THAT level - not of EL2 because EL2 is what used to be
;    read. Printing the wrong register's name beside a right-looking
;    value is the worst of the available mistakes.
;  * CPUECTLR_EL1.SMPEN, the coherency prerequisite - READ ONLY AT EL3.
;    It is implementation-defined and may trap below EL3, and this board
;    has no exception vector installed, so a trap is a silent hang. The
;    read is behind a runtime branch on the measured level for that
;    reason and for no other.
;  * The four firmware spin slots, read. Nothing in this tree has ever
;    reported what the stock stub leaves in them on this board.
;  * That the three board-reserved secondary stack pages are readable
;    and what they currently hold.
;  * THE PRIMARY-SIDE WAIT CONTRACT, EXECUTED AGAINST THE REAL COUNTER:
;    a WALL-CLOCK deadline off CNTPCT_EL0/CNTFRQ_EL0 rather than a spin
;    count, an acquire load of the state word, and a per-run NONCE that
;    the publisher must echo. Three self-tests run it against this
;    program's own word: one that never becomes ready (must time out),
;    one that becomes ready with this run's nonce (must succeed), and one
;    that becomes ready carrying SOMEBODY ELSE'S nonce (must refuse).
;
;  WHY THE NONCE. `CoreRawWaitReady()` in the library today accepts a
;  state word equal to READY, and its bound is a SPIN COUNT. Two things
;  follow. A spin count is not a time: the same loop with the caches off
;  is orders of magnitude slower than with them on, so one constant
;  cannot mean one deadline - that is the shape forum 726 already
;  records for the I2C driver. And a word is not a witness: a row left
;  READY by an earlier run of the same image, in memory nothing cleared,
;  reads exactly like a core that has just come up. The nonce makes the
;  second unsayable and the counter makes the first measurable.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, AND THE LIST IS THE POINT
; ======================================================================
;  Nothing about a second core. Not that one exists, not that one is
;  parked, not that one can be woken, not cache coherency, not WFE/SEV,
;  not the publication ordering as the hardware observes it, not
;  parallelism, not the EL3 stub. This is the PRECONDITION half. The
;  release half needs the EL3 stub on the boot medium and a permanent
;  worker inside the resident monitor, and neither exists today.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE OUTPUT, AND HOW TO READ IT
; ======================================================================
;  There is no serial adapter on this bench, so this writes a report
;  block into its own BSS and returns the block's ADDRESS in x0. The
;  monitor prints a returning payload's x0. Then:
;
;      memory <the address x0 printed> 100
;
;  The first word AND the last word are the magic $4357495441424C45,
;  which is "CWITABLE" in ASCII. If either is missing the block was not
;  written through, and nothing between them is this program's - which is
;  exactly why a magic is written at both ends rather than one.
;
;  Arm the deadman first, as every payload on this bench does:
;      deadman 15
;  The three wait self-tests are bounded at #CW_TEST_MS each, so the
;  whole program is a few milliseconds plus the reads.
; ======================================================================

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"

; --- status, in x0's report word 2 ------------------------------------
#CW_OK_WOULD_ADMIT   = 0   ; EL3, M/C/I set, SMPEN set: a release would be
                           ; admissible. NOTHING WAS RELEASED.
#CW_ERR_NOT_EL3      = 1   ; the machine is not at EL3
#CW_ERR_NO_MCI       = 2   ; at EL3, but M, C and I are not all set
#CW_ERR_NO_SMPEN     = 3   ; at EL3 with M/C/I, but CPUECTLR_EL1.SMPEN clear
#CW_ERR_NO_CLOCK     = 4   ; CNTFRQ_EL0 is not positive - no deadline exists
#CW_ERR_WAIT_BROKEN  = 5   ; the wait contract failed one of its self-tests

; --- the wait contract's own return codes -----------------------------
#CW_WAIT_READY       = 1   ; READY, and the nonce is this run's
#CW_WAIT_TIMEOUT     = 0   ; the deadline passed and it never became ready
#CW_WAIT_NO_CLOCK    = -4  ; CNTFRQ_EL0 is not positive
#CW_WAIT_BAD_BOUND   = -10 ; a non-positive deadline was asked for
#CW_WAIT_STALE       = -13 ; READY, carrying a nonce from another run

#CW_STATE_IDLE       = 0
#CW_STATE_READY      = 2

#CW_TEST_MS          = 5
#CW_REPORT_WORDS     = 32
#CW_MAGIC            = $4357495441424C45   ; "CWITABLE"

; the firmware's AArch64 spin table, read only
#CW_SPIN0            = $D8
#CW_SPIN1            = $E0
#CW_SPIN2            = $E8
#CW_SPIN3            = $F0

; The board's reserved raw-secondary stacks. These are the numbers
; RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4 declares; they are repeated as constants
; rather than reached through that file because including a BOARD file in
; a payload would also bring HwMonLo()/HwMonHi(), which measure whichever
; image contains them - here, this payload. A wrong monitor extent is
; worse than a repeated constant, and
; tools/multicore_witness_check.py checks these three against the board
; file on every run so the repetition cannot drift.
#CW_STACK_LO         = $001FC000
#CW_STACK_PAGE       = $00001000
#CW_STACK_CORES      = 3

; SCTLR M | C | I, the same three bits at EL2 and EL3 on ARMv8.0
#CW_SCTLR_MCI        = $1005
#CW_SMPEN            = $40

Global Dim cw_report.i[#CW_REPORT_WORDS]

; The word a secondary WOULD publish, and the nonce it WOULD echo. Both
; live in this program's own BSS: no secondary exists, and the self-tests
; drive them from this core so the wait contract is exercised rather than
; described.
Global cw_state.i
Global cw_nonce.i
Global cw_acqaddr.i

Procedure cwPut(slot.i, value.i)
  If slot >= 0 And slot < #CW_REPORT_WORDS
    cw_report[slot] = value
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The machine, read. Each of these is one MRS and nothing else.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cwCurrentEl()
  ASM
    mrs x0, currentel
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cwSctlrEl2()
  ASM
    mrs x0, sctlr_el2
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cwSctlrEl3()
  ASM
    mrs x0, sctlr_el3
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED AND EL3-ONLY IN PRACTICE. Every caller must have
; measured the level first; see the header.
Procedure.i cwCpuectlrEl1()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cpuectlr_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cwMpidrEl1()
  ASM
    mrs x0, mpidr_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cwEl()
  ProcedureReturn (cwCurrentEl() >> 2) & 3
EndProcedure

; The SCTLR OF THE LEVEL WE ARE AT. At EL3 an `mrs sctlr_el2` is legal
; and answers a register the processor is not using, so reading the wrong
; one produces a plausible number and no fault at all.
Procedure.i cwSctlr()
  If cwEl() = 3
    ProcedureReturn cwSctlrEl3()
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cwSctlrEl2()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE WAIT CONTRACT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The state word is consumed with an acquire load, so every witness the
;  publisher stored before its release is ordered before the reads that
;  follow here. The nonce is read after that acquire, which is what makes
;  it a witness of THIS run rather than of the memory's history.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cwLoadAcquire()
  ASM
    adrp x0, global_cw_acqaddr
    add  x0, x0, #:lo12:global_cw_acqaddr
    ldr  x0, [x0]
    ldar x0, [x0]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cwStateAcquire()
  cw_acqaddr = @cw_state
  ProcedureReturn cwLoadAcquire()
EndProcedure

; ms is a WALL-CLOCK bound off the architectural counter, not a spin
; count. A spin count means different amounts of time with the caches on
; and off, so it cannot be a deadline; this can.
Procedure.i CoreWitnessWait(ms.i, nonce.i)
  Define hz.i
  Define start.i
  Define limit.i
  If ms <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_BAD_BOUND
  EndIf
  hz = TickHz()
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_NO_CLOCK
  EndIf
  limit = (hz * ms) / 1000
  If limit <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_BAD_BOUND
  EndIf
  start = Ticks()
  Repeat
    If cwStateAcquire() = #CW_STATE_READY
      If cw_nonce = nonce
        ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_READY
      EndIf
      ; READY, and not from this run. Reporting success here is the
      ; exact failure this procedure exists to make impossible.
      ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_STALE
    EndIf
  Until Ticks() - start >= limit
  ProcedureReturn #CW_WAIT_TIMEOUT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Main()
  Define i.i
  Define el.i
  Define sctlr.i
  Define ectlr.i
  Define hz.i
  Define nonce.i
  Define t0.i
  Define t1.i
  Define r.i
  Define status.i
  Define base.i

  For i = 0 To #CW_REPORT_WORDS - 1
    cw_report[i] = 0
  Next

  TimerInit()
  t0 = Ticks()

  el = cwEl()
  sctlr = cwSctlr()
  hz = TickHz()

  cwPut(0, #CW_MAGIC)
  cwPut(1, 1)                       ; report version
  cwPut(3, cwCurrentEl())
  cwPut(4, el)
  cwPut(5, sctlr)
  ; -1 means NOT READ, which is a different fact from "read as zero".
  ectlr = -1
  If el = 3
    ectlr = cwCpuectlrEl1()
  EndIf
  cwPut(6, ectlr)
  cwPut(7, cwMpidrEl1())
  cwPut(8, hz)

  cwPut(9, PeekI(#CW_SPIN0))
  cwPut(10, PeekI(#CW_SPIN1))
  cwPut(11, PeekI(#CW_SPIN2))
  cwPut(12, PeekI(#CW_SPIN3))

  For i = 0 To #CW_STACK_CORES - 1
    base = #CW_STACK_LO + i * #CW_STACK_PAGE
    cwPut(13 + i * 3, base)
    cwPut(14 + i * 3, PeekI(base))
    cwPut(15 + i * 3, PeekI(base + #CW_STACK_PAGE - 8))
  Next

  ; --- the three wait self-tests ---------------------------------------
  ; The nonce is this run's, taken from the counter, so a report left in
  ; memory by a previous run cannot satisfy this one.
  nonce = t0 | 1
  cwPut(28, nonce)

  ; 1. never ready: must reach the deadline and say so.
  cw_state = #CW_STATE_IDLE
  cw_nonce = 0
  t1 = Ticks()
  r = CoreWitnessWait(#CW_TEST_MS, nonce)
  cwPut(22, r)
  cwPut(23, Ticks() - t1)

  ; 2. ready, carrying this run's nonce: must succeed.
  cw_nonce = nonce
  cw_state = #CW_STATE_READY
  t1 = Ticks()
  r = CoreWitnessWait(#CW_TEST_MS, nonce)
  cwPut(24, r)
  cwPut(25, Ticks() - t1)

  ; 3. READY, carrying somebody else's nonce: must REFUSE. This is a word
  ;    that says a core came up, in memory nothing cleared, from a run
  ;    that is over.
  cw_nonce = nonce + 1
  cw_state = #CW_STATE_READY
  t1 = Ticks()
  r = CoreWitnessWait(#CW_TEST_MS, nonce)
  cwPut(26, r)
  cwPut(27, Ticks() - t1)

  ; --- the verdict ------------------------------------------------------
  status = #CW_OK_WOULD_ADMIT
  If hz <= 0
    status = #CW_ERR_NO_CLOCK
  ElseIf cw_report[22] <> #CW_WAIT_TIMEOUT Or cw_report[24] <> #CW_WAIT_READY Or cw_report[26] <> #CW_WAIT_STALE
    status = #CW_ERR_WAIT_BROKEN
  ElseIf el <> 3
    status = #CW_ERR_NOT_EL3
  ElseIf (sctlr & #CW_SCTLR_MCI) <> #CW_SCTLR_MCI
    status = #CW_ERR_NO_MCI
  ElseIf (ectlr & #CW_SMPEN) = 0
    status = #CW_ERR_NO_SMPEN
  EndIf
  cwPut(2, status)

  ; Leave the word IDLE. A witness that left its own state word saying
  ; READY would be the stale word test 3 refuses, written by hand.
  cw_state = #CW_STATE_IDLE
  cw_nonce = 0

  cwPut(29, t0)
  cwPut(30, Ticks())
  cwPut(31, #CW_MAGIC)

  ; x0 is the report block's address. `memory <that> 100` reads it.
  ProcedureReturn @cw_report[0]
EndProcedure
